# Scaffolding & Layer Boundaries

Dependencies point inward: `presentation -> application -> domain <- infrastructure`.

## Hard rules
- UI / presentation code MUST NOT import database clients, ORMs, queue clients, or raw HTTP clients. It calls application services or use cases.
- Domain code (entities, value objects, domain services) MUST NOT import frameworks, I/O, or infrastructure. It is pure and unit-testable.
- Infrastructure (repositories, API clients, adapters) implements interfaces defined by the application/domain layer; it is never imported by presentation.
- Route handlers, controllers, and screens stay thin: parse input, call one service, map the result. No business logic.
- No circular imports. If two modules need each other, extract the shared piece into `shared/` or invert the dependency behind an interface.
- Features / modules do not reach into each other's internals; they use the public entry point (index file, package API, or interface).

## Placement
- Follow the folder architecture documented in `AGENTS.md`. Do not invent new top-level folders; ask first.
- One responsibility per file. Prefer small, named modules over `utils` or `helpers` dumping grounds.
- Shared code goes in `shared/` only after a second real use.

## Before finishing a change
- Check each new import against the direction above.
- Add or update tests next to the layer you changed (domain: unit; infrastructure: integration).
